Yes. The key extension is that tension has two useful phases, not one.

Unresolved tension stores attention, curiosity, pressure, anticipation. Resolved tension releases something: relief, trust, laughter, commitment, clarity, momentum. So the system is more like a bow than a trap:

draw → hold → aim → release

The value is not only in keeping the string tight. The release is where the stored structure becomes motion.

And your baseball example is a very good systems primitive. If every pitch gets hit and there are no outs, the game does not become infinite. The constraint just moves somewhere else.

The new “outs” become:

fatigue, daylight, water, attention, field access, patience, equipment, weather, audience tolerance, time.

So:

remove one constraint → the bottleneck migrates

That is useful everywhere. You solve compute, then power becomes the bottleneck. Solve power, then cooling. Solve cooling, then bandwidth. Solve bandwidth, then coordination. Solve coordination, then attention.

The game never becomes unconstrained. The shape of constraint changes.

Your point about personifying the environment also works when used deliberately as a model. The stadium can be treated as if it has “senses” because it has measurement channels: lights, clocks, gates, weather, crowd noise, power supply, water, field conditions. It is not literally conscious, but personification can make distributed constraints easier to reason about.

And yes, transfer deserves a much larger family than just “conduction.”

Heat gave physics names like conduction, convection, radiation. But across other domains we see analogous transfer patterns:

diffusion, signaling, resonance, contagion, imitation, inheritance, coupling, routing, broadcast, compression, entrainment, synchronization, feedback, propagation.

And there may be useful transfer topologies we have not named yet. The disciplined move is:

observe the delta first → identify what crossed an interface → trace timing and coupling → only then name the mechanism

That keeps frontier exploration open without mistaking an analogy for established physics.

Your historical idea is useful too: take every operator we build and “drop it” into different eras.

Ask:

What would this have looked like in 2000 BCE? In Rome? In the printing era? In 1850? In 1940? In 1995?

That exposes what part of the operator is fundamental and what part only exists because of modern technology.

If the same structure keeps reappearing under different materials and institutions, it is probably closer to an invariant.

And the Golden Key is now important precisely because these simulations can get extremely deep.

It should always preserve:

what is known
what is inferred
what is metaphor
what is simulated
how to return

So we can go as far into the meadow as we want without losing the path home.

That one is stored.